    </div>

    <!-- Overfitting Problem -->
    <section class="section" id="overfitting">
      <span class="section-label">Foundation Concepts</span>
      <h2>Understanding Overfitting</h2>
      <p>Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too well, including its noise and specific quirks, while failing to generalize to new data. An overfitted model has memorized the training set rather than learning underlying patterns.</p>

      <p>Imagine fitting a polynomial to 10 data points. A degree-2 polynomial might miss some points but describe a general trend. A degree-9 polynomial passes through every single point perfectly but has wild oscillations between them. When you get new data, the degree-9 polynomial performs terribly because it learned the noise, not the signal.</p>

      <p>The solution is constrained models that are forced to remain simple. Regularization adds penalties to the loss function that discourage complex models, creating a bias toward simplicity.</p>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> All successful machine learning models balance simplicity (low complexity) with accuracy (good training performance). This is the bias-variance tradeoff.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- L1 and L2 Regularization -->
    <section class="section" id="regularization">
      <span class="section-label">Core Techniques</span>
      <h2>L1 and L2 Regularization</h2>
      <p>Regularization adds a penalty to the loss function based on model complexity. The two most common approaches are L1 (Lasso) and L2 (Ridge) regularization.</p>

      <p><strong>L2 Regularization (Ridge):</strong> Adds penalty = λ × (sum of squared coefficients). This penalizes large weights, encouraging the model to distribute importance across many features rather than relying on a few. Ridge doesn't eliminate features; it shrinks their coefficients.</p>

      <p><strong>L1 Regularization (Lasso):</strong> Adds penalty = λ × (sum of absolute coefficients). This can shrink some coefficients all the way to zero, performing feature selection automatically. L1 is useful when you suspect many features are irrelevant.</p>

      <h3>Implementing L1 and L2 Regularization</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">linear_model</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">Ridge</span>, <span class="fn">Lasso</span>, <span class="fn">ElasticNet</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">cross_val_score</span>

<span class="cm"># L2 Regularization (Ridge)</span>
<span class="va">ridge</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">Ridge</span>(<span class="dt">alpha</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1.0</span>)  <span class="cm"># Higher alpha = more regularization</span>
<span class="va">ridge</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Ridge test score: {ridge.score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)

<span class="cm"># L1 Regularization (Lasso)</span>
<span class="va">lasso</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">Lasso</span>(<span class="dt">alpha</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.1</span>)
<span class="va">lasso</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Lasso test score: {lasso.score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Lasso selected {(lasso.coef_ != 0).sum()} features"</span>)

<span class="cm"># ElasticNet: combines L1 and L2</span>
<span class="va">elastic</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">ElasticNet</span>(<span class="dt">alpha</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.1</span>, <span class="dt">l1_ratio</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.5</span>)
<span class="va">elastic</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"ElasticNet test score: {elastic.score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>

      <div class="callout tip">
        <strong>When to use each:</strong> Use Ridge for regression when all features might be useful. Use Lasso for feature selection when you have many features. Use ElasticNet for a balance between the two.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Early Stopping -->
    <section class="section" id="early-stopping">
      <span class="section-label">Core Techniques</span>
      <h2>Early Stopping</h2>
      <p>Early stopping is particularly useful for iterative models (neural networks, gradient boosting) that improve gradually. The idea is simple: monitor validation performance during training. If it stops improving, stop training immediately to prevent overfitting.</p>

      <p>As epochs progress, training error decreases continuously, but validation error eventually starts increasing as the model overfits. Early stopping stops right when validation error starts increasing.</p>

      <h3>Early Stopping in Gradient Boosting</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">GradientBoostingClassifier</span>

<span class="cm"># Create model with early stopping</span>
<span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">GradientBoostingClassifier</span>(
    <span class="dt">n_estimators</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1000</span>,  <span class="cm"># Max iterations</span>
    <span class="dt">validation_fraction</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.2</span>,  <span class="cm"># Use 20% for validation</span>
    <span class="dt">n_iter_no_change</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">10</span>,  <span class="cm"># Stop if no improvement for 10 iterations</span>
    <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Train (will stop early)</span>
<span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Stopped after {model.n_estimators_} iterations"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Test score: {model.score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Dropout and Ensemble -->
    <section class="section" id="dropout-ensemble">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span>
      <h2>Dropout and Ensemble Methods</h2>
      <p><strong>Dropout</strong> is a technique primarily used in neural networks. During training, random neurons are "dropped" (set to zero) with some probability p. This forces the network to learn redundant representations that don't rely on any single neuron. It's like training many models with different subsets of neurons, then averaging them.</p>

      <p><strong>Ensemble Methods</strong> train multiple models and average their predictions. Random forests, gradient boosting, and bagging all work by building many trees/models and combining them. Ensemble methods reduce overfitting through diversity: different models overfit in different ways, so averaging cancels out the overfitting.</p>

      <p>The key insight: if your models overfit independently (to different noise in the data), their errors cancel out when averaged. This is why ensemble methods are among the most robust techniques in machine learning.</p>

      <h3>Implementing Ensemble Methods</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">RandomForestClassifier</span>, <span class="fn">GradientBoostingClassifier</span>, <span class="fn">VotingClassifier</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">linear_model</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">LogisticRegression</span>

<span class="cm"># Individual models</span>
<span class="va">rf</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">RandomForestClassifier</span>(<span class="dt">n_estimators</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>)
<span class="va">gb</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">GradientBoostingClassifier</span>(<span class="dt">n_estimators</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>)
<span class="va">lr</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">LogisticRegression</span>(<span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>)

<span class="cm"># Voting ensemble (average predictions)</span>
<span class="va">voting_clf</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">VotingClassifier</span>(
    <span class="va">estimators</span><span class="op">=</span>[(<span class="st">'rf'</span>, <span class="va">rf</span>), (<span class="st">'gb'</span>, <span class="va">gb</span>), (<span class="st">'lr'</span>, <span class="va">lr</span>)],
    <span class="dt">voting</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'soft'</span>  <span class="cm"># soft = average probabilities</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Train and evaluate</span>
<span class="va">voting_clf</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Voting ensemble test score: {voting_clf.score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)

<span class="cm"># Compare to individual models</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"RF test score: {rf.fit(X_train, y_train).score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"GB test score: {gb.fit(X_train, y_train).score(X_test, y_test):.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Validation Curves -->
    <section class="section" id="validation-curves">
      <span class="section-label">Diagnosis Tools</span>
      <h2>Validation Curves to Diagnose Overfitting</h2>
      <p>Validation curves show how training and validation performance change as you vary a hyperparameter. They reveal whether increasing/decreasing a hyperparameter helps with overfitting or underfitting.</p>

      <p>If the validation curve plateaus while training continues improving, you have overfitting. If both curves remain low, you have underfitting. The goal is to find the sweet spot where validation performance is maximized.</p>

      <h3>Plotting Validation Curves</h3>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">validation_curve</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">matplotlib</span>.<span class="im">pyplot</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">plt</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Test varying regularization strength</span>
<span class="va">param_range</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">logspace</span>(<span class="nm">-6</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">10</span>)
<span class="va">train_scores</span>, <span class="va">val_scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">validation_curve</span>(
    <span class="fn">Ridge</span>(),
    <span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>,
    <span class="dt">param_name</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'alpha'</span>,
    <span class="dt">param_range</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">param_range</span>,
    <span class="dt">cv</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">5</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Plot</span>
<span class="va">train_mean</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">train_scores</span>, <span class="dt">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)
<span class="va">val_mean</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">val_scores</span>, <span class="dt">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)

<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">plot</span>(<span class="va">param_range</span>, <span class="va">train_mean</span>, <span class="dt">label</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'Training'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">plot</span>(<span class="va">param_range</span>, <span class="va">val_mean</span>, <span class="dt">label</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'Validation'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">xscale</span>(<span class="st">'log'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">xlabel</span>(<span class="st">'Alpha (Regularization Strength)'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">ylabel</span>(<span class="st">'Score'</span>)
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">legend</span>()
<span class="im">plt</span>.<span class="fn">show</span>()</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Complete Anti-Overfitting Strategy -->
    <section class="section" id="strategy">
      <span class="section-label">Real-World Application</span>
      <h2>Complete Anti-Overfitting Strategy</h2>
      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">model_selection</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">GridSearchCV</span>, <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">GradientBoostingRegressor</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn</span>.<span class="im">preprocessing</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="fn">StandardScaler</span>

<span class="cm"># 1. Data splitting with stratification</span>
<span class="va">X_temp</span>, <span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_temp</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">train_test_split</span>(
    <span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">y</span>, <span class="dt">test_size</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.2</span>, <span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>
)

<span class="cm"># 2. Standardize features (important for regularized models)</span>
<span class="va">scaler</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">StandardScaler</span>()
<span class="va">X_temp_scaled</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">scaler</span>.<span class="fn">fit_transform</span>(<span class="va">X_temp</span>)
<span class="va">X_test_scaled</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">scaler</span>.<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>)

<span class="cm"># 3. Tune hyperparameters with cross-validation</span>
<span class="va">param_grid</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">'learning_rate'</span>: [<span class="nm">0.01</span>, <span class="nm">0.1</span>],
    <span class="st">'max_depth'</span>: [<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">7</span>],
    <span class="st">'subsample'</span>: [<span class="nm">0.8</span>, <span class="nm">0.9</span>],  <span class="cm"># Stochastic gradient boosting</span>
    <span class="st">'n_estimators'</span>: [<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">200</span>]
}

<span class="va">gs</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">GridSearchCV</span>(
    <span class="fn">GradientBoostingRegressor</span>(<span class="dt">random_state</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">42</span>),
    <span class="va">param_grid</span>,
    <span class="dt">cv</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">5</span>,
    <span class="dt">n_jobs</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">-1</span>
)
<span class="va">gs</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_temp_scaled</span>, <span class="va">y_temp</span>)

<span class="cm"># 4. Final evaluation (only once!)</span>
<span class="va">final_model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">gs</span>.<span class="va">best_estimator_</span>
<span class="va">test_r2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">final_model</span>.<span class="fn">score</span>(<span class="va">X_test_scaled</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span>)
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Final test R²: {test_r2:.3f}"</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

